kosha organic-varga-extended

  -- undergraduate organic chemistry: the systematic study of carbon compounds.
  -- the grammar of organic chemistry has three levels:
  --
  --   1. backbone: the carbon skeleton — chain length, branching, rings, saturation.
  --      alkane (CnH2n+2) → alkene (CnH2n) → alkyne (CnH2n-2) → aromatic (benzene ring)
  --      the backbone determines: boiling point, solubility in water, shape.
  --
  --   2. functional groups: the reactive sites on the backbone.
  --      -OH (alcohol), -COOH (acid), -NH2 (amine), C=O (carbonyl),
  --      -COO- (ester), -CONH- (amide), -SH (thiol), -X (halide).
  --      the functional group determines: reactivity, what reactions are possible.
  --
  --   3. stereochemistry: the 3D arrangement of atoms — chirality, cis/trans.
  --      same formula, same connectivity, different 3D shape → different molecules.
  --      chirality: carbon with 4 different groups — L/D amino acids, R/S sugars.
  --      cis/trans: double bonds lock rotation.
  --
  -- the four reaction types that connect everything:
  --   addition:     A + B → C  (alkenes, carbonyls)
  --   elimination:  C → A + B  (dehydration, E2)
  --   substitution: A + BC → AC + B  (SN1, SN2)
  --   oxidation/reduction: change in C oxidation state
  --
  -- aromaticity: benzene ring C6H6 — 6 π electrons delocalised over ring.
  -- Hückel rule: 4n+2 π electrons → aromatic. special stability.
  -- benzene, naphthalene, pyridine, pyrrole, furan, thiophene.
  -- aromatic amino acids: phenylalanine, tyrosine, tryptophan.
  -- DNA bases: purines (adenine, guanine) and pyrimidines (cytosine, thymine) are aromatic.

  "organic-varga-vishesa"
